<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svantevit</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Svetovid, Sventovit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svantevit was a god of the Baltic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, worshipped above all by the Rani at the temple fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> on Rügen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a> describes his huge wooden statue with four heads holding a drinking horn, a sacred white horse kept for divination, and tribute sent from other Slavic lands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> destroyed the image in 1168. He is usually linked with war, plenty and prophecy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svantevit.jpg" alt="Svantevit" width="320" height="594" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: W. Dollese; Derivative work: Sławobóg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svetovit.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
